{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba p1a/AUDIT.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# P1a AUDIT — Gambit 4.9.8 runtime: the mechanisms the jolt FFI contract needs\n     2\t\n     3\t- Auditor: glm-5.3-flash (VM Runtime Engineer delegate), P1a slice, Gambit FFI track\n     4\t- Date: 2026-11-18 (+07)\n     5\t- Ruling followed: DOC-FIRST (owner ruling 2026-11-18) — manual chapter/section\n     6\t  first, then the documented C API in the installed headers, runtime\n     7\t  implementation `file:line` only where the docs underdetermine the pinned\n     8\t  semantics, with the doc gap named.\n     9\t- Scope: audit + witnesses ONLY. No jolt adapter code was written.\n    10\t\n    11\t## Sources cited\n    12\t\n    13\t- `[txi §...]` = the official manual shipped in the source clone,\n    14\t  `~/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src/doc/gambit.txi` (line numbers of the current\n    15\t  checkout, tag v4.9.8, commit 2228e4d).\n    16\t- `[gambit.h:N]` = the installed configured header\n    17\t  `~/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/include/gambit.h`.\n    18\t- `[lib/file.c:N]` = `~/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src/lib/...` (implementation\n    19\t  tier, cited only to resolve a documented gap).\n    20\t- Witness outputs are recorded verbatim in `p1a/witnesses/results/*.out`; each\n    21\t  run is under the external watchdog `p1a/witnesses/watchdog.sh` (SIGKILL +\n    22\t  exit 124 on timeout, so a deadlock is a recorded failure).\n    23\t- Provisioned toolchain: `~/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/bin/{gsi,gsc}`\n    24\t  (absolute paths only; bare `gsc` on PATH is Ghostscript). The mise plugin\n    25\t  (`~/.local/share/mise/plugins/gambit/lib/utils.bash:68`) builds with exactly\n    26\t  `./configure --enable-single-host --prefix=...` — nothing else.\n    27\t\n    28\t---\n    29\t\n    30\t## Q1 — Runtime build configuration and threading model of the provisioned 4.9.8\n    31\t\n    32\t**Answer: single VM, single OS-threaded (`___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS`), one\n    33\tprocessor (green threads multiplexed over exactly one OS thread that runs all\n    34\tScheme execution); `--enable-single-host` codegen; NO multi-processor support\n    35\tcompiled in. The jolt CI flags `f8,-8,t8` are I/O-encoding defaults only and\n    36\thave no threading effect. The provisioned runtime differs from CI's runtime\n    37\tonly in those baked default I/O options (CI's build had them compiled in via\n    38\t`--enable-default-runtime-options`; mise's build uses the library defaults).**\n    39\t\n    40\tEvidence, in the DOC-FIRST order:\n    41\t\n    42\t1. Manual:\n    43\t   - Threads are green: a thread is \"a virtual processor which shares object\n    44\t     space with all other threads\" [txi §Threads/Introduction, gambit.txi:9876].\n    45\t     Execution starts on the \"primordial thread\"; \"All threads are terminated\n    46\t     when the primordial thread terminates\" [txi §Threads/Primordial thread,\n    47\t     gambit.txi:10105-10114]. Nothing in the Threads chapter mentions OS-level\n    48\t     threads or processors; the model presented is one object space\n    49\t     multiplexed by the scheduler.\n    50\t2. Documented headers (installed):\n    51\t   - The configured header pins the threading model:\n    52\t     `#define ___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS` [gambit.h:1469-1472]. That line is the\n    53\t     configure substitution of `@CONF_SINGLE_MULTIPLE_THREADED_VMS@`\n    54\t     [gambit-v4.9.8-src/include/gambit.h.in:1471], which is `___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS`\n    55\t     unless `--enable-multiple-threaded-vms` is passed [configure:6348-6357;\n    56\t     configure.ac:1271-1283 default `no`]. The mise plugin passes only\n    57\t     `--enable-single-host`, so the provisioned build has the default.\n    58\t   - `___MAX_PROCESSORS` = 1 [gambit.h:1489-1493; configure:6394-6398 sets\n    59\t     `CONF_MAX_PROCESSORS=1` unless multiple-threaded-vms; the 64 maximum\n    60\t     applies only to that build].\n    61\t   - The comment at [gambit.h:1464-1466] states the semantics: \"Are Gambit VM\n    62\t     instances single or multiple threaded at the OS level? In other words,\n    63\t     is it allowed for multiple OS threads to be running in a given VM\n    64\t     instance?\" — the provisioned answer is NO.\n    65\t   - POSIX thread system is linked (`___USE_POSIX_THREAD_SYSTEM`,\n    66\t     [gambit.h:1498-1505]), but that only selects the locking/primitives\n    67\t     implementation; it does not create a second Scheme-executing OS thread.\n    68\t   - `--enable-single-host` is codegen only: \"compile each Scheme module as a\n    69\t     single C function (by default each Scheme procedure is compiled to a C\n    70\t     function, otherwise N ... is the maximum number of control points ...)\"\n    71\t     [configure:1778-1784]. It reaches the build as `-D___SINGLE_HOST`\n    72\t     [configure:5116-5119] and the installed `gambuild-C` passes it to every\n    73\t     compiled module (`DEFS_OBJ=\" -D___SINGLE_HOST \"`, bin/gambuild-C:27). It\n    74\t     has no threading implication.\n    75\t3. Implementation (resolves what the docs do not say about the processor\n    76\t   count at startup): a VM \"starts off with a single processor\":\n    77\t   `___vms->processor_count = 1; ___vms->mem.target_processor_count_ = 1;`\n    78\t   [lib/setup.c:4791-4795]. The documented resize entry clamps any request to\n    79\t   `___MAX_PROCESSORS` [gambit.h:14767-14770 declares `___current_vm_resize`;\n    80\t   lib/setup.c:1163-1166 clamps; the resize machinery itself is compiled out\n    81\t   entirely under `___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS`, lib/setup.c:884-1013]. The\n    82\t   documented garbage-collect entry is a barrier sync over processors\n    83\t   [gambit.h:14772-14775; lib/setup.c:1181-1194 via `on_all_processors`].\n    84\t4. CI's `--enable-default-runtime-options=f8,-8,t8`: per the manual's Runtime\n    85\t   options chapter, `f` is `file-settings=` (gambit.txi:2106), `-` is\n    86\t   `stdio-settings=` (gambit.txi:2109), `t` is `terminal-settings=`\n    87\t   (gambit.txi:2121), and the code `8` selects \"UTF-8 character encoding\"\n    88\t   (gambit.txi:2356-2357) among the per-port default I/O settings\n    89\t   (gambit.txi:2319-2370). So `f8,-8,t8` forces UTF-8 as the default\n    90\t   character encoding for file, stdio and terminal ports — a pure I/O\n    91\t   setting. It is baked into the binaries at configure time as\n    92\t   `___DEFAULT_RUNTIME_OPTIONS` (configure:5412-5424), consumed first among\n    93\t   the option sources before `GAMBOPT`/script/`-:` [lib/main.c:24-25,\n    94\t   505-620]. **It has zero effect on threading, GC, or the FFI.** The\n    95\t   provisioned runtime matches CI's threading configuration exactly (both are\n    96\t   `___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS`/1-processor builds; CI added no\n    97\t   `--enable-multiple-threaded-vms` either); the only divergence is the baked\n    98\t   UTF-8 default encodings.\n    99\t\n   100\t**Witness W0 (build-config, PASS)** — `p1a/witnesses/w0-config.scm`, output\n   101\t`results/w0.out` (verbatim):\n   102\t\n   103\t```\n   104\t### cmd: ./watchdog.sh 60 ./w0-config.exe\n   105\t=== W0 build-configuration witness ===\n   106\t___VERSION=409008\n   107\t___SINGLE_VM=defined\n   108\t___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS=defined\n   109\t___MAX_PROCESSORS=1\n   110\t___USE_POSIX_THREAD_SYSTEM=defined\n   111\t___SINGLE_HOST=defined (-D by gambuild-C)\n   112\t___SINGLE_HOST_LIMIT=30000\n   113\t___USE_RC_ALLOCATION_FOR_FOREIGN=defined\n   114\tmin-heap-bytes=2097152\n   115\tmax-heap-bytes=0\n   116\tlive-percent=50\n   117\tparallelism-level=1\n   118\t##cpu-count=8\n   119\t##core-count=8\n   120\t##current-vm-processor-count=1\n   121\tafter ##current-vm-resize(4): processor-count=1 (before: 1)\n   122\tW0-VERDICT: PASS (configuration recorded)\n   123\t### exit=0\n   124\t```\n   125\t\n   126\tThe last three lines are the empirical proof on the provisioned binaries:\n   127\t8 hardware cores exist, the VM runs ONE processor, and the documented resize\n   128\trequest for 4 processors is clamped back to 1.\n   129\t\n   130\t---\n   131\t\n   132\t## Q2 — The exact leave/re-enter (release/claim) mechanism\n   133\t\n   134\t**Answer: no such mechanism exists in Gambit 4.9.8 — not in the manual, not\n   135\tin the documented C API, and not in the runtime implementation. There is no\n   136\tentry point by which the code inside a foreign call releases the processor\n   137\tfor other threads and re-claims it afterwards.**\n   138\t\n   139\t- Doc tier 1 (manual): the entire C-interface chapter (gambit.txi:18672-19993)\n   140\t  documents c-declare/c-initialize/c-lambda/c-define/c-define-type and the\n   141\t  nesting section. No release/claim/suspend/resume of runtime ownership is\n   142\t  documented anywhere. The nearest documented behavior is the WARNING that\n   143\t  nested C→Scheme calls are unsafe across threads (see Q3/Q4).\n   144\t- Doc tier 2 (headers): `gambit.h` has no `___leave`/`___enter`/`claim`\n   145\t  execution-ownership API (grep negative). The only exported \"release\" entry\n   146\t  points are OBJECT releases — `___release_rc`, `___release_scmobj`,\n   147\t  `___release_pointer`, `___release_string`, ... (gambit.h:9840-9886,\n   148\t  10179-10268) — reference-count releases for foreign data, unrelated to\n   149\t  execution ownership. The exported runtime-control API is exactly:\n   150\t  `___setup_params_reset`, `___setup`, `___cleanup`,\n   151\t  `___cleanup_and_exit_process`, `___current_vm_resize`, `___garbage_collect`,\n   152\t  `___setup_vmstate`/`___cleanup_vmstate`/`___setup_pstate`/`___cleanup_pstate`\n   153\t  [gambit.h:14757-14788] — none of them releases the current processor while\n   154\t  staying inside a foreign frame.\n   155\t- Implementation tier (gap justified: the docs do not determine whether an\n   156\t  undocumented mechanism exists): grep of `lib/*.c` for\n   157\t  `___leave/___claim/LEAVE_GAMBIT/ENTER_GAMBIT/___reclaim` returns nothing.\n   158\t  The processor hand-off points that DO exist are only (a) the scheduler's\n   159\t  own green-thread switch (inside Scheme execution, at safe points — never\n   160\t  inside a foreign call) and (b) the multi-processor sync ops\n   161\t  (`barrier_sync_op`, lib/setup.c:542+) — which are compiled to no-ops under\n   162\t  `___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS` (lib/setup.c:527-541, 549) and would require a\n   163\t  second processor anyway. A c-lambda/c-define body runs inline on the\n   164\t  owning processor for its whole duration; the generated entry/exit macros\n   165\t  (gambit.h:10028-10044 `___BEGIN_SFUN_*` / `___END_SFUN_*`; `___BEGIN_CFUN_*`\n   166\t  in gambit.h:10327-10360) contain no ownership transition.\n   167\t\n   168\tConsequence for the jolt blocking transition (astra §2 steps 4 and 6 — \"Leave\n   169\truntime execution through the verified mechanism\" / \"Re-enter the runtime\n   170\tthrough its required claim mechanism\"): **there is no verified mechanism to\n   171\tuse.**\n   172\t\n   173\t---\n   174\t\n   175\t## Q3 — Does a released foreign frame permit (a) another Gambit thread to run and (b) collection to complete?\n   176\t\n   177\t**Answer: the question's premise (a \"released foreign frame\") cannot be\n   178\tconstructed on this build — there is no release (Q2). Moreover, even if C\n   179\tcode simply blocks without releasing anything, NO other Gambit thread can run\n   180\tand NO collection can complete, for two independent structural reasons:**\n   181\t\n   182\t1. **One OS thread executes everything.** With `___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS` and\n   183\t   `___MAX_PROCESSORS=1` (Q1), all green threads are multiplexed by the\n   184\t   scheduler onto the single OS thread that entered the runtime. While that\n   185\t   OS thread is inside a foreign call, the scheduler is not running, so no\n   186\t   other green thread can be dispatched. There is no second OS thread that\n   187\t   could run them.\n   188\t2. **No release-on-block exists** (Q2), so the blocked foreign call occupies\n   189\t   the only processor until it returns.\n   190\t3. **GC cannot run either.** Collection is driven by the running thread: at\n   191\t   allocation points inside Scheme execution, or explicitly through\n   192\t   `(##gc)` — whose primitive body calls the documented C API\n   193\t   `___garbage_collect` (gambit.h:14772-14775; lib/_kernel.scm:2032-2069) —\n   194\t   which is a synchronous barrier op over all processors\n   195\t   (lib/setup.c:1181-1194, `on_all_processors`). With the only thread blocked\n   196\t   in C, no allocation-driven GC happens and no thread exists to call\n   197\t   `___garbage_collect`. The manual's own documentation of the C-interface\n   198\t   corroborates the serialization: \"This situation can be avoided by having\n   199\t   only one Scheme thread that does Scheme to C to Scheme calls. Other Scheme\n   200\t   threads are limited to simple Scheme to C calls that don't call back to\n   201\t   Scheme\" [txi §Continuations, the C-interface and threads,\n   202\t   gambit.txi:19980-19991].\n   203\t\n   204\tMechanism summary (what makes it true/false): `___GET_REAL_PSTATE()` returns\n   205\tthe ONE fixed processor state `&___GSTATE->vmstate0.aligned_pstate[0].pstate`\n   206\tand `___SET_REAL_PSTATE(ps)` is a no-op under `___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS`\n   207\t[gambit.h:8606-8612] — there is exactly one execution context in the process\n   208\tand no way to create another.\n   209\t\n   210\t**Witness W2 (blocking-GC) — control PASS, blocked FAIL (decisive NO-GO\n   211\tevidence for the `sa-foreign-procedure-blocking` row on this build).**\n   212\t`p1a/witnesses/w2-blocking.scm` + `w2-fixture.c`; outputs\n   213\t`results/w2-control.out`, `results/w2-blocked.out`.\n   214\t\n   215\t- Control (non-blocking foreign calls + real GC from another thread): PASS.\n   216\t  The runtime's own `*** GC:` report lines (enabled by `-:d5`, \"At level 5\n   217\t  and above garbage collection reports are displayed during program\n   218\t  execution\", gambit.txi:2236-2238) are the verbatim proof that REAL\n   219\t  collections ran — the adapter's no-op `sa-gc-collect` cannot produce them\n   220\t  (host/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss:81, `sa-gc-collect` returns `#f`).\n   221\t\n   222\t```\n   223\tW2-B: round 1 t=1795020328.917097 allocating + ##gc (runtime primitive)\n   224\t*** GC: 507us, 93K alloc, 6.5M heap, 1.6M live (24% 69936+1607840)\n   225\tW2-B: round 1 t=1795020328.918424 done (capi-gc-return=0)\n   226\t...\n   227\tW2-A: non-blocking foreign call 1 t=1795020328.920896\n   228\tW2C: non-blocking foreign call entered+returned\n   229\t...\n   230\tW2-VERDICT: PASS (control: real collections completed while another thread made foreign calls; the *** GC: report lines above are the runtime's own collection evidence)\n   231\t### exit=0\n   232\t```\n   233\t\n   234\t- Blocked: FAIL, exactly as predicted. Thread B is made runnable 0.3 s after\n   235\t  thread A enters the blocking foreign call (6 s C-side deadline). During the\n   236\t  entire blocked window B produces ZERO lines and ZERO `*** GC:` reports; B's\n   237\t  rounds all appear after `W2C: foreign call RETURNING`:\n   238\t\n   239\t```\n   240\tW2: t=1795020329.854461 start\n   241\tW2C: foreign call ENTERED, blocking on handshake (deadline 6s)\n   242\tW2C: foreign call RETURNING after blocked interval\n   243\tW2-B: round 1 t=1795020335.854959 allocating + ##gc (runtime primitive)\n   244\t*** GC: 834us, 93K alloc, 6.5M heap, 1.6M live (24% 69472+1607840)\n   245\t...\n   246\tW2: blocked window from t=1795020329.854498 to t=1795020335.862667\n   247\tW2-VERDICT: FAIL (blocked: no collection and no other-thread progress while the foreign call was blocked — every W2-B line above appears after 'W2C: foreign call RETURNING'; the VM has a single processor and 4.9.8 has no foreign-release mechanism, so the blocking row cannot be satisfied on this build)\n   248\t### exit=0\n   249\t```\n   250\t\n   251\tThe ~6.0 s gap between the blocked window start (…329.854) and B's first\n   252\tround (…335.854) is the starvation measured directly. A Scheme-internal\n   253\twatchdog cannot fire in this state (the whole VM is frozen), which is why\n   254\tevery run is wrapped in the external `watchdog.sh`; here the C-side deadline\n   255\tproduced the structured verdict, and `exit=0` is the witness program's own\n   256\texit, not a pass of the gate.\n   257\t\n   258\t---\n   259\t\n   260\t## Q4 — Rules for a previously unknown pthread entering Scheme\n   261\t\n   262\t**Answer: there is NO documented protocol, and there is no usable mechanism\n   263\teither. A previously unknown pthread may not run Scheme code on this build:\n   264\tthe empirically observed outcome of naive entry is a deterministic SIGSEGV\n   265\t(and nondeterministic corruption for concurrent entry).**\n   266\t\n   267\t- Doc tier 1 (manual): no protocol is documented. The c-define section\n   268\t  documents only the single-threaded \"server\" pattern: initialize with\n   269\t  `___setup` from the driving thread, terminate with `___cleanup`\n   270\t  (gambit.txi:19448-19451; sample tests/server.scm + tests/client.c). The\n   271\t  nesting section explicitly restricts multi-thread use: only ONE Scheme\n   272\t  thread may perform Scheme→C→Scheme calls, others only simple Scheme→C calls\n   273\t  (gambit.txi:19980-19991) — it says nothing about foreign OS threads.\n   274\t- Doc tier 2 (headers): under `___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS` there is no way to\n   275\t  associate a second OS thread with the runtime:\n   276\t  `___GET_REAL_PSTATE()` returns the fixed global pstate and\n   277\t  `___SET_REAL_PSTATE(ps)` is a NO-OP (gambit.h:8606-8612) — a foreign thread\n   278\t  cannot even be bound to a processor state. `___setup_pstate`\n   279\t  (gambit.h:14782-14784) exists, but on this build no second pstate can be\n   280\t  created: the only creation path, `___vm_resize_pstate`, is compiled out\n   281\t  (lib/setup.c:866-1013, body under `#ifndef ___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS`), and\n   282\t  `___current_vm_resize` clamps to 1 (lib/setup.c:1163-1166). The generated\n   283\t  c-define entry macros require a current processor state with no ownership\n   284\t  check (`___processor_state ___ps=___PSTATE;` — gambit.h:10028-10044), so a\n   285\t  foreign pthread that calls a c-define'd function silently shares the\n   286\t  primordial's pstate.\n   287\t- Implementation tier (the crash mechanism — gap justified: the docs do not\n   288\t  describe what happens; the crash explains WHY no protocol is offered):\n   289\t  the C→Scheme entry `___call` arms a setjmp catcher ON THE CALLING THREAD'S\n   290\t  C STACK (`___BEGIN_TRY` sets `___ps->catcher = &___jbuf` with `___jbuf` a\n   291\t  local — gambit.h:9966-9971) and, on the Scheme→C return, the return-to-C\n   292\t  handler jumps back \"inside ___call\" with\n   293\t  `___throw_error(___UNWIND_C_STACK)` (lib/_kernel.c:11772). The unwind\n   294\t  target is the SHARED pstate's catcher chain, and the cross-processor guard\n   295\t  in that handler is compiled out under `___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS`\n   296\t  (lib/_kernel.c:11742-11755). After `___setup` returns on the primordial,\n   297\t  the outermost catcher of that chain is NULL (`___setup_pstate` initializes\n   298\t  `___ps->catcher = 0`, lib/setup.c:4755; `___run`'s catcher is a stack local\n   299\t  restored on return, lib/setup.c:3389-3415). The observed macOS crash report\n   300\t  for W3 phase1 matches exactly:\n   301\t\n   302\t```\n   303\texception: EXC_BAD_ACCESS, SIGSEGV, KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS at 0x00000000000000b0\n   304\t  libsystem_platform.dylib  longjmp\n   305\t  w3.exe  ___call\n   306\t  w3.exe  scheme_w3_add\n   307\t  w3.exe  w3_worker_serialized\n   308\t  libsystem_pthread.dylib  _pthread_start\n   309\t```\n   310\t\n   311\t  — the foreign thread's entry faults inside `longjmp` reading the NULL\n   312\t  (0xb0 ≈ jmpbuf field offset) or foreign-stack catcher.\n   313\t\n   314\t**Witness W3 (foreign-thread entry) — FAIL on every mode.**\n   315\t`p1a/witnesses/w3-fthread.c` + `w3-server.scm` (the manual's documented\n   316\t\"Scheme server\" build pattern, tests/makefile.in:166); outputs\n   317\t`results/w3-phase1.out`, `w3-phase1-nobeat.out`, `w3-phase2-run1.out`,\n   318\t`w3-phase2-run2.out`, `w3-phase3.out`.\n   319\t\n   320\t- phase1 — raw pthread calls c-define'd Scheme procedures after `___setup`,\n   321\t  primordial parked in `pthread_join` (serialized, heartbeat on): **SIGSEGV,\n   322\t  exit 139, deterministically (3/3 runs across the session)**, before the\n   323\t  worker's first Scheme call completes (no `W3-WORKER` line is ever printed):\n   324\t\n   325\t```\n   326\tW3: ___setup returned; runtime live (___VERSION=409008)\n   327\tW3-PHASE1: spawning raw pthread (primordial will park in pthread_join)\n   328\tW3-PHASE1: heartbeat interval = 0.010000\n   329\t./watchdog.sh: line 21:  1511 Segmentation fault: 11  \"$@\"\n   330\t### exit=139\n   331\t```\n   332\t\n   333\t- phase1-nobeat — same, with the heartbeat DISABLED via the undocumented\n   334\t  `___set_heartbeat_interval(0)` (lib/os_time.c:937-948; used only to isolate\n   335\t  the crash cause): **still SIGSEGV, exit 139** — the crash is not the\n   336\t  heartbeat interrupt.\n   337\t\n   338\t```\n   339\tW3-PHASE1: heartbeat DISABLED for this run\n   340\t./watchdog.sh: line 21:  1532 Segmentation fault: 11  \"$@\"\n   341\t### exit=139\n   342\t```\n   343\t\n   344\t- phase2 — concurrent entry (worker calls Scheme while the primordial thread\n   345\t  runs an allocating Scheme loop): **nondeterministic undefined behavior —\n   346\t  observed exit 139 (SIGSEGV) in one session and exit 70 (silent runtime\n   347\t  fatal exit, no message) twice in the recorded session; outcomes vary\n   348\t  between runs of the same binary.**\n   349\t\n   350\t```\n   351\tW3-PHASE2: spawning raw pthread WHILE primordial runs scheme_w3_spin\n   352\t### exit=70\n   353\t```\n   354\t\n   355\t- phase3 — owner Gambit thread parked inside a cfun (c-lambda) call while the\n   356\t  worker enters (the shape under which W4-M4's callback survived): **still\n   357\t  SIGSEGV, exit 139** — even the discriminating allocating entry\n   358\t  (`scheme_w3_add_alloc`) never completes:\n   359\t\n   360\t```\n   361\tW3-PHASE3: owner Gambit thread parks INSIDE a cfun call while the worker pthread enters Scheme\n   362\t./watchdog.sh: line 21:  1590 Segmentation fault: 11  \"$@\"\n   363\t### exit=139\n   364\t```\n   365\t\n   366\t**Verdict for the contract row:** an unrelated C thread may NOT enter Scheme\n   367\ton the provisioned 4.9.8 build. NO-GO for callbacks from an unrelated C\n   368\tthread; the refusal row stays.\n   369\t\n   370\t---\n   371\t\n   372\t## Q5 — Root and processor-state obligations across leave/re-enter transitions\n   373\t\n   374\t**Answer: there are no leave/re-enter transitions to service (Q2). The\n   375\tobligations that DO exist are the ones around the two documented crossing\n   376\tdirections — Scheme→C (cfun/c-lambda) and C→Scheme (sfun/c-define) — and they\n   377\tare all documented; they are also exactly why an uncoordinated foreign thread\n   378\tcannot enter (no rooted context of its own).**\n   379\t\n   380\t1. **Processor-state propagation through conversions.** Every conversion\n   381\t   helper takes the current processor state and callers must propagate it\n   382\t   with the `___PSD`/`___PSP` macros: \"Note that the use of the `___PSD` macro\n   383\t   in the parameter list of `SCMOBJ_to_POINT` and the `___PSP` macro in the\n   384\t   calls ... are necessary to propagate the current processor state to that\n   385\t   function\" [txi §c-define-type example, gambit.txi:19726-19731]. The\n   386\t   generated c-define entry pins this contract in the header:\n   387\t   `___BEGIN_SFUN_*(proc)` begins with `___processor_state ___ps=___PSTATE;`\n   388\t   [gambit.h:10028-10044]. There is no way to pass a DIFFERENT pstate — the\n   389\t   current one is fixed (gambit.h:8606-8612).\n   390\t2. **What must be rooted, and for how long.**\n   391\t   - C→Scheme results allocate STILL objects on the C heap, reference-counted:\n   392\t     \"`___SCMOBJ ___make_pair(___processor_state ___ps, ___SCMOBJ car,\n   393\t     ___SCMOBJ cdr);` ... A `NULL` `___ps` parameter will allocate the pair\n   394\t     permanently ... Otherwise a 'still' object is allocated and the\n   395\t     `___ps` parameter indicates the processor in whose heap the object is\n   396\t     allocated ... Still objects are reference counted and initially have a\n   397\t     reference count equal to 1. The call to `___release_scmobj` in the\n   398\t     macros `___END_CFUN_..._to_SCMOBJ` and `___END_SFUN_..._to_SCMOBJ`\n   399\t     decrement this reference count. A still object whose reference count is\n   400\t     zero will be deallocated when a garbage collection is performed and\n   401\t     there are no references to it from the Scheme world.\" [txi\n   402\t     gambit.txi:19706-19724]. The corresponding documented macros:\n   403\t     `___alloc_rc`/`___release_rc` used by every struct/union/type\n   404\t     conversion (gambit.h:9814-9876, `___USE_RC_ALLOCATION_FOR_FOREIGN`).\n   405\t   - The C→Scheme ENTRY must root the called procedure for the duration of\n   406\t     the call: `___make_sfun_stack_marker` creates a heap vector holding the\n   407\t     procedure, killed by `___kill_sfun_stack_marker` after the call\n   408\t     (gambit.h:10028-10044; lib/c_intf.c:6511-6556; the marker is the\n   409\t     \"unwind destination\" the return-to-C handler checks,\n   410\t     lib/_kernel.c:11741-11772). The TODO in\n   411\t     lib/c_intf.c:6528-6529 (\"proc_or_false may have been GC'd at this\n   412\t     point! protect it some way\") documents the sharp edge the macro family\n   413\t     papers over.\n   414\t   - Scheme→C: the lifetime rule is that pointers to Scheme objects passed\n   415\t     into C are valid only for the call's duration; C must not keep them.\n   416\t     The manual documents the conversion-temporary contract (\"The temporary\n   417\t     memory allocated from the C heap for the conversion of the arguments\n   418\t     and result will be reclaimed whether there is an error or not\", txi\n   419\t     §c-lambda, gambit.txi:19296-19300) and the `___AT_END` hook as the\n   420\t     cleanup point after the result is converted (gambit.txi:19313-19319).\n   421\t     The foreign-type `release-function` is invoked \"at most once ... When\n   422\t     the garbage collector detects that a foreign object is no longer\n   423\t     reachable ... it will invoke the release-function\" (gambit.txi:19022-19036).\n   424\t   - Object identity across GC: the manual's release-function semantics are\n   425\t     the documented rooting story for foreign objects; there is NO documented\n   426\t     pinning API (relevant to `sa-lock-object`: the CONTRACT's non-moving\n   427\t     collector exemption does not transfer — nothing in 4.9.8 documentation\n   428\t     or headers pins a moving Scheme object).\n   429\t3. **What must be saved/restored.** For the two documented directions, the\n   430\t   generated macros do it all: cfun entry saves the Scheme registers to\n   431\t   memory and restores them on exit (`___BEGIN_CFUN_CTX` = `___SET_STK`\n   432\t   + `___W_ALL` ... `___END_CFUN_CTX` = `___R_ALL`, gambit.h:10310-10311), and\n   433\t   the sfun entry builds/kills the stack marker (gambit.h:10028-10044). There\n   434\t   is nothing for an integrator to save manually — and nothing the\n   435\t   integrator CAN save manually, because the catcher/stack context is\n   436\t   per-thread C-stack state held in the shared pstate (gambit.h:9966-9972;\n   437\t   lib/setup.c:4755) — precisely the state a foreign pthread cannot\n   438\t   legitimately own (Q4).\n   439\t\n   440\t**Witness W1 (errno-atomicity, PASS)** — `p1a/witnesses/w1-errno.scm` +\n   441\t`w1-fixture.c`; output `results/w1.out`. Exercises the conversion-boundary\n   442\tobligations above (the astra essential sequence) plus nesting (the\n   443\tdocumented one-thread C→Scheme→C→Scheme) and two-thread interleaving:\n   444\t\n   445\t```\n   446\t### cmd: ./watchdog.sh 120 ./w1.exe\n   447\t=== W1 errno-atomicity witness ===\n   448\tEAGAIN=35 EACCES=13 EIO=5\n   449\tW1-CHECKS=4000\n   450\tW1-FAILURES=0\n   451\tW1-VERDICT: PASS (result+errno pair captured atomically at the shim; capture order proven by post-capture and ___AT_END clobbers; per-invocation capture proven by nesting and threads)\n   452\t### exit=0\n   453\t```\n   454\t\n   455\tMechanisms exercised: typed C shims capturing (result, errno) immediately\n   456\tafter the target (shapes A/B/C, 200 each); success preserving errno=42 (no\n   457\tnormalization); deliberate post-capture clobber (errno=7777) in every shim;\n   458\t`___AT_END` clobber AFTER result conversion (shape D, 100); nested\n   459\tre-entry capture (shape E, 100 — outer pair captured before the C→Scheme→C\n   460\tround trip must still be (−3, EIO)); 2 Scheme threads × 400 interleaved\n   461\titerations each (a global \"last errno\" cell or deferred re-read fails these;\n   462\tper-invocation capture passes). 4000/4000 assertions correct on macOS arm64.\n   463\t\n   464\t---\n   465\t\n   466\t## Witness W4 (callables) — modes that work and modes that do not\n   467\t\n   468\t`p1a/witnesses/w4-callables.scm` + `w4-fixture.c`; outputs\n   469\t`results/w4-mode1.out`, `w4-mode4-run{1,2,3}.out`, `w4-mode5.out`.\n   470\t\n   471\t- **M1 same-thread callback (C fixture → c-define'd Scheme proc): PASS.**\n   472\t  `w4_call_cb(4) -> 40` — the documented c-define path (gambit.txi:19396-19451).\n   473\t- **M2 nested callbacks C→Scheme→C→Scheme on one thread: PASS.**\n   474\t  `w4_nested_a(1) -> 1107` (chain a(1)→p(1)→b(2)→q(2)→…→1107). Documented as\n   475\t  allowed for ONE thread (gambit.txi:19936-19991).\n   476\t- **M3 callback from a second Gambit thread \"during\" a released blocking\n   477\t  call: structurally impossible on this build.** Demonstrated: thread A\n   478\t  blocks 2 s inside the foreign call, thread B attempts the same callback\n   479\t  during the block — B's attempt and completion occur only AFTER A's call\n   480\t  returned (probe `B-completed-during-A-block = 0`; B's log line t=…337.834\n   481\t  vs A's return t=…337.534). Verdict PASS only in the serialized sense; the\n   482\t  jolt requirement (progress during the foreign call) is covered by W2's FAIL.\n   483\t- **M4 callback from a raw foreign pthread: PASS-QUALIFIED, NOT a supported\n   484\t  mode.** The callback returned `1005` in 3/3 runs when the owner Gambit\n   485\t  thread was parked inside the cfun call (serialized reuse of the single\n   486\t  pstate). But the structurally closest sibling — W3 phase3, same owner-parked\n   487\t  shape, worker calling a c-define'd procedure — SIGSEGVs 1/1, and W3 phase1\n   488\t  SIGSEGVs 3/3. Survival here is an undocumented artifact of the exact\n   489\t  frame state, not a contract: no protocol exists (Q4), nothing roots the\n   490\t  worker's context, and the same witness family crashes one shape over.\n   491\t  P1b must NOT advertise this mode; the refusal row stays.\n   492\t- **M5 exception raised inside a callback: the process dies.** The error\n   493\t  escapes the callback, prints `*** ERROR IN w4-m5 -- w4-cb-raise:\n   494\t  deliberate exception 3` and exits with status 70 — the C caller gets no\n   495\t  error code and no chance to handle it. Consequence for P1b: callback error\n   496\t  handling must be INSIDE the callback body (with-exception-catcher), which\n   497\t  is exactly what the manual's own server example does\n   498\t  (`catch-all-errors`, tests/server.scm:15-19) — a bounded implementation\n   499\t  should make this part of the callable contract; an uncaught callback\n   500\t  exception must be treated as a host-fatal event, not a recoverable error.\n   501\t\n   502\t```\n   503\t### cmd: ./watchdog.sh 60 ./w4.exe mode5\n   504\t=== W4 mode5: M5 exception inside a callback ===\n   505\tW4-M5: calling w4_call_raise(3) — callback raises a Scheme error\n   506\t*** ERROR IN w4-m5 -- w4-cb-raise: deliberate exception 3\n   507\t### exit=70\n   508\t```\n   509\t\n   510\t---\n   511\t\n   512\t## Other contract-relevant findings\n   513\t\n   514\t1. **`(##gc)` is the real collection primitive** for the adapter's\n   515\t   `sa-gc-collect` upgrade path: lib/_kernel.scm:2051-2069 calls the\n   516\t   documented `___garbage_collect` (gambit.h:14772-14775). The adapter's\n   517\t   current no-op (host/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss:81-82) can be\n   518\t   replaced by `(##gc)` — W2's control run shows it produces real collections\n   519\t   (the `*** GC:` report lines). This is safe within one processor's Scheme\n   520\t   execution; it does NOT solve anything about blocking calls (Q3).\n   521\t2. **`___setup_pstate`/`___setup_vmstate` are exported** (gambit.h:14776-14787)\n   522\t   but unusable for foreign threads on this build (Q4): no second pstate can\n   523\t   be created (resize compiled out), no TLS binding exists to install one.\n   524\t3. **Variadic C functions are not supported by c-lambda** (\"Variadic C\n   525\t   functions are not supported\", txi §c-lambda, gambit.txi:19297) — relevant\n   526\t   to the jolt varargs-specialization path (astra's construction-path\n   527\t   inventory).\n   528\t4. **errno observation:** W1 proves the errno pair can be captured\n   529\t   atomically in typed shims on this build (macOS errno values:\n   530\t   EAGAIN=35, EACCES=13, EIO=5; the witness probes them at runtime so the\n   531\t   assertions hold on Linux too).\n   532\t5. **Watchdog requirement is structural:** a blocked foreign call freezes the\n   533\t   whole VM (W2 blocked), so no in-process timeout can ever fire — every\n   534\t   jolt-level witness driver that calls foreign code must be watchdogged\n   535\t   externally (the Makefile does this for every run).\n   536\t\n   537\t---\n   538\t\n   539\t## GO / NO-GO recommendation for P1b (astra §5 criteria, verbatim application)\n   540\t\n   541\t**NO-GO for P2/P3 — and PARTIAL-GO is available only for the basic tier.**\n   542\tApplying astra's exact wording:\n   543\t\n   544\t- \"**NO-GO for P2/P3:** collection cannot complete while C is blocked\" —\n   545\t  PROVEN by W2 (blocked: zero collection, zero other-thread progress over the\n   546\t  6 s blocked window, on the pinned runtime). This criterion is met, and it\n   547\t  alone forces NO-GO for P2/P3, because \"P2 remains Chez-only if P1 blocking\n   548\t  is unqualified\" and \"Removing `:blocking` from crypto calls is not an\n   549\t  acceptable workaround\".\n   550\t- \"**unrelated C-thread entry cannot be made safe where required**\" — PROVEN\n   551\t  by W3 (deterministic SIGSEGV on serialized entry; nondeterministic\n   552\t  SIGSEGV/exit-70 on concurrent entry; crash mechanism rooted in the shared\n   553\t  pstate catcher chain, gambit.h:9966-9972 + lib/_kernel.c:11741-11772 +\n   554\t  gambit.h:8606-8612).\n   555\t- \"**exact runtime ownership rules remain unknown**\" — they are now known,\n   556\t  and they say: one pstate, one OS thread, no release/claim, no foreign\n   557\t  thread protocol. That knowledge closes the audit but confirms the rows\n   558\t  cannot be qualified on this build.\n   559\t- The worker-pool escape is pre-ruled by astra (\"A worker pool is **not** a\n   560\t  transparent generic fallback ... cannot masquerade as unrestricted\n   561\t  `sa-foreign-procedure-blocking`\") — and W3/W4 additionally show that even\n   562\t  moving a call to another pthread does not give that thread a valid Scheme\n   563\t  context (SIGSEGV), so the pool would have to run calls on the SAME (only)\n   564\t  OS thread, which is precisely the serialization that defeats blocking.\n   565\t- **Partial GO (astra's fallback ruling):** \"memory, loading, ordinary calls,\n   566\t  and errno may land independently when qualified.\" W1 (errno) PASSes; the\n   567\t  ordinary-call tier is exercised throughout W1/W2-control/W4-M1/M2 via\n   568\t  c-lambda/c-define with correct results; the memory/loader tier was not\n   569\t  separately qualified here (dlopen/sa-foreign-alloc are NOT part of P1a's\n   570\t  witness list — they are P1b implementation targets; nothing observed\n   571\t  contradicts them, but they are unqualified by this audit).\n   572\t\n   573\t**Bottom line:** the pinned Gambit 4.9.8 (mise default configure,\n   574\t`--enable-single-host`, `___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS`, 1 processor) can support the\n   575\tjolt FFI tier's basic surface (typed shims with atomic errno capture, ordinary\n   576\tnon-blocking calls, callbacks on the owning thread, real `(##gc)`), but it\n   577\tCANNOT support `sa-foreign-procedure-blocking`'s contract (other threads' GC\n   578\tmust proceed while C is blocked) or callbacks from unrelated C threads, and\n   579\tthere is no runtime mechanism that could be used to build them. Per astra's\n   580\tfallback ruling, those constructors must keep raising their message-carrying\n   581\tunsupported errors and `CONTRACT.txt`/`TARGET-CONTRACT.md` must describe the\n   582\tpartial mode. The next decision on those rows is an explicit Gambit upgrade\n   583\tor runtime patch through the upgrade gate (e.g. a build with\n   584\t`--enable-multiple-threaded-vms` would still need a verified foreign-thread\n   585\tattach protocol, which upstream does not document even for that build).\n   586\t\n   587\t### Reproducing\n   588\t\n   589\t```\n   590\tcd ~/workspace/jolt-ffi-p1/p1a/witnesses\n   591\tmake clean && make all        # builds every witness, runs each under watchdog.sh, records results/*.out\n   592\t```\n   593\t\n   594\tWitness sources: `w0-config.scm`, `w1-errno.scm` + `w1-fixture.c`,\n   595\t`w2-blocking.scm` + `w2-fixture.c`, `w3-fthread.c` + `w3-server.scm`,\n   596\t`w4-callables.scm` + `w4-fixture.c`, `watchdog.sh`, `Makefile`. Recorded\n   597\tverbatim outputs: `results/*.out` (one file per run, `### exit=` per run).\n   598\tToolchain: `/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/bin/gsc`\n   599\t(never bare `gsc`; that is Ghostscript).\n   600\t\n   601\t### Open risks / limitations of this audit\n   602\t\n   603\t- Witnesses ran on macOS arm64 (darwin 25.6.0). astra requires both macOS\n   604\t  arm64 and Linux x86_64 for the pinned rows; the Linux leg is unexecuted in\n   605\t  P1a. The structural evidence (configured header, compiled-out resize,\n   606\t  shared-pstate catcher) is platform-independent, but the crash signatures\n   607\t  (SIGSEGV in longjmp, exit-70) should be re-recorded on Linux before any\n   608\t  document update that cites them.\n   609\t- W3-phase2/W4-M4 outcomes are nondeterministic/fragile by nature; the\n   610\t  recorded outputs are one sample of a nondeterministic distribution (two\n   611\t  observed exit codes for phase2 across sessions). The deterministic rows\n   612\t  (phase1/phase3 SIGSEGV) were reproduced 3/3 and 1/1 respectively.\n   613\t- The out-of-tree differently-configured Gambit build (p1a/gambit-builds/)\n   614\t  was NOT needed and NOT built: the audit's verdicts follow from the\n   615\t  provisioned configuration itself; a multiple-threaded-vms build would not\n   616\t  change the missing-protocol conclusion.\n   617\t- `make clean` regenerates everything; committed sources only (no binaries,\n   618\t  no generated .c files).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
