{c-define} special form makes it possible to create a C\n 19402\tfunction that will act as a representative of some Scheme procedure.  A\n 19403\tC function named @i{c-name} as well as a Scheme procedure bound to the\n 19404\tvariable @i{variable} are defined.  The parameters of the Scheme\n 19405\tprocedure are @i{define-formals} and its body is at the end of the\n 19406\tform.  The type of each argument of the C function, its result type and\n 19407\t@i{c-name} (which must be a string) are specified after the parameter\n 19408\tspecification of the Scheme procedure.  When the C function @i{c-name}\n 19409\tis called from C, its arguments are converted to their Scheme\n 19410\trepresentation and passed to the Scheme procedure.  The result of the\n 19411\tScheme procedure is then converted to its C representation and the C\n 19412\tfunction @i{c-name} returns it to its caller.\n 19413\t\n 19414\tThe scope of the C function can be changed with the @i{scope}\n 19415\tparameter, which must be a string.  This string is placed immediately\n 19416\tbefore the declaration of the C function.  So if @i{scope} is the\n 19417\tstring @code{\"static\"}, the scope of @i{c-name} is local to the module\n 19418\tit is in, whereas if @i{scope} is the empty string, @i{c-name} is\n 19419\tvisible from other modules.\n 19420\t\n 19421\tThe @code{c-define} special form does not return a value.\n 19422\tIt can only appear at top level.\n 19423\t\n 19424\tFor example:\n 19425\t\n 19426\t@smallexample\n 19427\t@b{}(c-define (proc x #!optional (y x) #!rest z) (int int char float) int \"f\" \"\"\n 19428\t  (write (cons x (cons y z)))\n 19429\t  (newline)\n 19430\t  (+ x y))\n 19431\t\n 19432\t(proc 1 2 #\\x 1.5) => 3 and prints (1 2 #\\x 1.5)\n 19433\t(proc 1)           => 2 and prints (1 1)\n 19434\t\n 19435\t; if f is called from C with the call  f (1, 2, 'x', 1.5)\n 19436\t; the value 3 is returned and (1 2 #\\x 1.5) is printed.\n 19437\t; f has to be called with 4 arguments.\n 19438\t@end smallexample\n 19439\t\n 19440\t@findex ___setup\n 19441\t@findex ___cleanup\n 19442\tThe @code{c-define} special form is particularly useful when the\n 19443\tdriving part of an application is written in C and Scheme procedures\n 19444\tare called directly from C.  The Scheme part of the application is in\n 19445\ta sense a ``server'' that is providing services to the C part.  The\n 19446\tScheme procedures that are to be called from C need to be defined\n 19447\tusing the @code{c-define} special form.  Before it can be used, the\n 19448\tScheme part must be initialized with a call to the function\n 19449\t@samp{___setup}.  Before the program terminates, it must call the\n 19450\tfunction @samp{___cleanup} so that the Scheme part may do final\n 19451\tcleanup.  A sample application is given in the file\n 19452\t@samp{tests/server.scm}.\n 19453\t\n 19454\t@end deffn\n 19455\t\n 19456\t@node c-define-type, continuations, c-define, C-interface\n 19457\t@section The @code{c-define-type} special form\n 19458\t@findex c-define-type\n 19459\t\n 19460\t@deffn {special form} c-define-type @r{@i{name}} @r{@i{type}} @r{[}@r{@i{c-to-scheme}} @r{@i{scheme-to-c}} @r{[}@r{@i{cleanup}}@r{]}@r{]}\n 19461\t\n 19462\tThis form associates the type identifier @i{name} to the C type\n 19463\t@i{type}.  The @i{name} must not clash with predefined types\n 19464\t(e.g. @code{char-string}, @code{ISO-8859-1}, etc.) or with types previously\n 19465\tdefined with @code{c-define-type} in the same file.  The\n10031:#define ___SFUN_ERROR_VOID while ((___err!=___FIX(___NO_ERR))&&(___err!=___FIX(___UNWIND_C_STACK))) {___ps->temp1=___err;___ps->temp2=___errdata;___ps->temp3=___marker;___ps->temp4=___errhandler;___err=___EXT(___call) (___PSP 0,___ps->handler_sfun_conv_error,___marker);\n10032:#define ___END_SFUN_VOID } ___EXT(___kill_sfun_stack_marker) (___marker); } ___EXT(___propagate_error) (___PSP ___err);\n10034:#define ___BEGIN_SFUN_SCMOBJ(proc) ___processor_state ___ps=___PSTATE;___SCMOBJ __