{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src/doc/gambit.txi | sed -n '18920,18990p;19000,19042p;19282,19328p;19688,19742p'; rg -n 'c.closure|C.closure|function type|function pointer|closure|nonmov|non-mov|still object|movable' /Users/sto/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src/doc/gambit.txi | tail -45\"","aggregated_output":" 18920\t@code{nonnull-UCS-2-string}\n 18921\t@item nonnull-UCS-2-string-list\n 18922\t@code{nonnull-UCS-2-string-list}\n 18923\t@item UCS-4-string\n 18924\t@code{UCS-4-string}\n 18925\t@item nonnull-UCS-4-string\n 18926\t@code{nonnull-UCS-4-string}\n 18927\t@item nonnull-UCS-4-string-list\n 18928\t@code{nonnull-UCS-4-string-list}\n 18929\t@item wchar_t-string\n 18930\t@code{wchar_t-string}\n 18931\t@item nonnull-wchar_t-string\n 18932\t@code{nonnull-wchar_t-string}\n 18933\t@item nonnull-wchar_t-string-list\n 18934\t@code{nonnull-wchar_t-string-list}\n 18935\t@item scheme-object\n 18936\t@code{scheme-object}\n 18937\t@item @var{name}\n 18938\tappropriate translation of @var{name} (where @var{name}\n 18939\tis a C type defined with @code{c-define-type})\n 18940\t@item \"@var{c-type-id}\"\n 18941\t@var{c-type-id} (this form is equivalent to @code{(type \"@var{c-type-id}\")})\n 18942\t@end table\n 18943\t\n 18944\tThe @code{struct}, @code{union}, @code{type}, @code{pointer} and\n 18945\t@code{nonnull-pointer} types are ``foreign types'' and they are\n 18946\trepresented on the Scheme side as ``foreign objects''.  A foreign object\n 18947\tis internally represented as a pointer.  This internal pointer is\n 18948\tidentical to the C pointer being represented in the case of the\n 18949\t@code{pointer} and @code{nonnull-pointer} types.\n 18950\t\n 18951\tIn the case of the @code{struct}, @code{union} and @code{type} types,\n 18952\tthe internal pointer points to a copy of the C data type being\n 18953\trepresented.  When an instance of one of these types is converted from C\n 18954\tto Scheme, a block of memory is allocated from the C heap and\n 18955\tinitialized with the instance and then a foreign object is allocated\n 18956\tfrom the Scheme heap and initialized with the pointer to this copy.\n 18957\tThis approach may appear overly complex, but it allows the conversion of\n 18958\tC++ classes that do not have a zero parameter constructor or an\n 18959\tassignment method (i.e. when compiling with a C++ compiler an instance\n 18960\tis copied using @samp{new @var{type} (@var{instance})}, which calls the\n 18961\tcopy-constructor of @var{type} if it is a class; @var{type}'s assignment\n 18962\toperator is never used).  Conversion from Scheme to C simply\n 18963\tdereferences the internal pointer (no allocation from the C heap is\n 18964\tperformed).  Deallocation of the copy on the C heap is under the control\n 18965\tof the release function attached to the foreign object (see below).\n 18966\t\n 18967\tThe optional @var{tags} field of foreign type specifications is used\n 18968\tfor type checking on the Scheme side.  The @var{tags} field must be\n 18969\t@code{#f}, a symbol or a non-empty list of symbols.  When it is not\n 18970\tspecified the @var{tags} field defaults to a symbol whose name, as\n 18971\treturned by @code{symbol->string}, is the C type declaration for that\n 18972\ttype.  For example the symbol @samp{char**} is the default for the\n 18973\ttype @samp{(pointer (pointer char))}.  A @var{tags} field that is a\n 18974\tsingle symbol is equivalent to a list containing only that symbol.\n 18975\tThe first symbol in the list of tags is the primary tag.  For example\n 18976\tthe primary tag of the type @samp{(pointer char)} is @samp{char*} and\n 18977\tthe primary tag of the type @samp{(pointer char (foo bar))} is\n 18978\t@samp{foo}.\n 18979\t\n 18980\tType compatibility between two foreign types depends on their tags.\n 18981\tAn instance of a foreign type @var{T} can be used where a foreign type\n 18982\t@var{E} is expected if and only if\n 18983\t\n 18984\t@itemize @bullet{}\n 18985\t\n 18986\t@item\n 18987\t@var{T}'s @var{tags} field is @code{#f}, or\n 18988\t\n 18989\t@item\n 18990\t@var{E}'s @var{tags} field is @code{#f}, or\n 19000\tprocedure) contains the primary tag (if the @var{tags} field is not\n 19001\t@code{#f}), and the hexadecimal address denoted by the internal\n 19002\tpointer, for example @samp{#<char** #2 0x2AAC535C>}.  Note that the\n 19003\thexadecimal address is in C notation, which can be easily transferred\n 19004\tto a C debugger with a ``cut-and-paste''.\n 19005\t\n 19006\tA @var{release-function} can also be specified within a foreign type\n 19007\tspecification.  The @var{release-function} must be @code{#f} or a string\n 19008\tnaming a C function with a single parameter of type @samp{void*} (in\n 19009\twhich the internal pointer is passed) and with a result of type\n 19010\t@samp{___SCMOBJ} (for returning an error code).  When the\n 19011\t@var{release-function} is not specified or is @code{#f} a default\n 19012\tfunction is constructed by the C-interface.  This default function does\n 19013\tnothing in the case of the @code{pointer} and @code{nonnull-pointer}\n 19014\ttypes (deallocation is not the responsibility of the C-interface) and\n 19015\treturns the fixnum @samp{___FIX(___NO_ERR)} to indicate no error.  In\n 19016\tthe case of the @code{struct}, @code{union} and @code{type} types, the\n 19017\tdefault function reclaims the copy on the C heap referenced by the\n 19018\tinternal pointer (when using a C++ compiler this is done using\n 19019\t@samp{delete (@var{type}*)@var{internal-pointer}}, which calls the\n 19020\tdestructor of @var{type} if it is a class) and returns\n 19021\t@samp{___FIX(___NO_ERR)}.  In many situations the default\n 19022\t@var{release-function} will perform the appropriate cleanup for the\n 19023\tforeign type.  However, in certain cases special operations (such as\n 19024\tdecrementing a reference count, removing the object from a table, etc)\n 19025\tmust be performed.  For such cases a user supplied\n 19026\t@var{release-function} is needed.\n 19027\t\n 19028\tThe @var{release-function} is invoked at most once for any foreign\n 19029\tobject.  After the @var{release-function} is invoked, the foreign\n 19030\tobject is considered ``released'' and can no longer be used in a\n 19031\tforeign type conversion.  When the garbage collector detects that a\n 19032\tforeign object is no longer reachable by the program, it will invoke\n 19033\tthe @var{release-function} if the foreign object is not yet released.\n 19034\tWhen there is a need to release the foreign object promptly, the\n 19035\tprogram can explicitly call @code{(foreign-release! @var{obj})} which\n 19036\tinvokes the @var{release-function} if the foreign object is not yet\n 19037\treleased, and does nothing otherwise.  The call\n 19038\t@code{(foreign-released? @var{obj})} returns a boolean indicating\n 19039\twhether the foreign object @var{obj} has been released yet or not.\n 19040\tThe call @code{(foreign-address @var{obj})} returns the address\n 19041\tdenoted by the internal pointer of foreign object @var{obj} or 0 if it\n 19042\thas been released.  The call @code{(foreign? @var{obj})} tests that\n 19282\t@end smallexample\n 19283\t\n 19284\t@end deffn\n 19285\t\n 19286\t@node c-lambda, c-define, c-initialize, C-interface\n 19287\t@section The @code{c-lambda} special form\n 19288\t@findex c-lambda\n 19289\t\n 19290\t@deffn {special form} c-lambda (@r{@i{type1}}@dots{}) @r{@i{result-type}} @r{@i{c-name-or-code}}\n 19291\t\n 19292\tThe @code{c-lambda} special form makes it possible to create a Scheme\n 19293\tprocedure that will act as a representative of some C function or C code\n 19294\tsequence.  The first subform is a list containing the type of each\n 19295\targument.  The type of the function's result is given next.  Finally,\n 19296\tthe last subform is a string that either contains the name of the C\n 19297\tfunction to call or some sequence of C code to execute.  Variadic C\n 19298\tfunctions are not supported.  The resulting Scheme procedure takes\n 19299\texactly the number of arguments specified and delivers them in the same\n 19300\torder to the C function.  When the Scheme procedure is called, the\n 19301\targuments will be converted to their C representation and then the C\n 19302\tfunction will be called.  The result returned by the C function will be\n 19303\tconverted to its Scheme representation and this value will be returned\n 19304\tfrom the Scheme procedure call.  An error will be signaled if some\n 19305\tconversion is not possible.  The temporary memory allocated from the C\n 19306\theap for the conversion of the arguments and result will be reclaimed\n 19307\twhether there is an error or not.\n 19308\t\n 19309\tWhen @i{c-name-or-code} is not a valid C identifier, it is treated as\n 19310\tan arbitrary piece of C code.  Within the C code the variables\n 19311\t@samp{___arg1}, @samp{___arg2}, etc. can be referenced to access the\n 19312\tconverted arguments.  Note that the C @code{return} statement can't be\n 19313\tused to return from the procedure.  Instead, the @code{___return}\n 19314\tmacro must be used.  A procedure whose @i{result-type} is not\n 19315\t@code{void} must pass the procedure's result as the single argument to\n 19316\tthe @code{___return} macro, for example @samp{___return(123);} to\n 19317\treturn the value 123.  When @i{result-type} is @code{void}, the\n 19318\t@code{___return} macro must be called without a parameter list, for\n 19319\texample @samp{___return;}.\n 19320\t\n 19321\tThe C code is copied to the C file on a new line so it can start with\n 19322\tpreprocessor directives.  Moreover the C code is always placed at the\n 19323\thead of a compound statement whose lifetime encloses the C to Scheme\n 19324\tconversion of the procedure's result.  Consequently, temporary storage\n 19325\t(strings in particular) declared at the head of the C code can be\n 19326\treturned with the @code{___return} macro.\n 19327\t\n 19328\tIn the @i{c-name-or-code}, the macro @samp{___AT_END} may be defined\n 19688\t\n 19689\tc-declare-end\n 19690\t)\n 19691\t\n 19692\t(c-define-type point \"point\" \"POINT_to_SCMOBJ\" \"SCMOBJ_to_POINT\" #f)\n 19693\t\n 19694\t(define line-to (c-lambda (point) void \"line_to\"))\n 19695\t(define get-mouse (c-lambda () point \"get_mouse\"))\n 19696\t(define add-points (c-lambda (point point) point \"add_points\"))\n 19697\t\n 19698\t(c-define (write-point p) (point) void \"write_point\" \"\"\n 19699\t  (write p))\n 19700\t\n 19701\t(c-define (read-point) () point \"read_point\" \"\"\n 19702\t  (read))\n 19703\t@end smallexample\n 19704\t\n 19705\tNote that the pair is allocated using the @code{___make_pair} runtime\n 19706\tlibrary function.  The prototype of this function is\n 19707\t\n 19708\t@smallexample\n 19709\t@b{}___SCMOBJ ___make_pair(___processor_state ___ps, ___SCMOBJ car, ___SCMOBJ cdr);\n 19710\t@end smallexample\n 19711\t\n 19712\tThe fields of the pair are initialized to the @code{car} and\n 19713\t@code{cdr} parameters.  The @code{___ps} parameter indicates how the\n 19714\tpair is allocated.  A @code{NULL} @code{___ps} parameter will allocate\n 19715\tthe pair permanently (i.e. the pair will only be deallocated when\n 19716\t@code{___cleanup} is called).  Otherwise a ``still'' object is\n 19717\tallocated and the @code{___ps} parameter indicates the processor in\n 19718\twhose heap the object is allocated (this is to support multithreaded\n 19719\texecution).  Still objects are reference counted and initially have a\n 19720\treference count equal to 1.  The call to @code{___release_scmobj} in\n 19721\tthe macros @code{___END_CFUN_POINT_to_SCMOBJ} and\n 19722\t@code{___END_SFUN_POINT_to_SCMOBJ} decrement this reference count.  A\n 19723\tstill object whose reference count is zero will be deallocated when a\n 19724\tgarbage collection is performed and there are no references to it from\n 19725\tthe Scheme world.  Note that the use of the @code{___PSD} macro\n 19726\tin the parameter list of @code{SCMOBJ_to_POINT} and the @code{___PSP} macro\n 19727\tin the calls of @code{SCMOBJ_to_POINT}, are necessary to propagate the\n 19728\tcurrent processor state to that function.\n 19729\t\n 19730\tAn example that requires memory allocation when converting from C to\n 19731\tScheme and Scheme to C is shown below.  It is an interface to a\n 19732\t``null-terminated array of strings'' type which is represented in Scheme\n 19733\tby a list of strings.  Note that some cleanup is necessary when\n 19734\tconverting from Scheme to C.\n 19735\t\n 19736\t@smallexample\n 19737\t@b{}(c-declare #<<c-declare-end\n 19738\t\n 19739\t#include <stdlib.h>\n 19740\t#include <unistd.h>\n 19741\t\n 19742\textern char **environ;\n3364:occupied by movable and nonmovable objects.\n4947:target and only up to a size that is allowed for @emph{movable\n5077:Force (or don't force) the creation of fresh closures when evaluating\n5078:lambda-expressions.  A fresh closure is always created when a\n9252:closures, contain strong references.  An object @var{X} is\n19723:still object whose reference count is zero will be deallocated when a\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
